{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791581113,"nonce":"+h8IQSwq0QfUVYII6tTa7YEBQiE=","title":"For: persistent keyed children from an iterator, with cleanup per row","message":"Wanted (user, 2026-10-09): the equivalent of ashui's For, taking iterators. Part of retiring stateful closures (2c565dc): a list that changes today means rebuilding the container's whole subtree. Show (a branch built when its condition holds and disposed when it stops) is the same machinery. Signal-bound when() (696122f2c37171297755be98b82ce84ad6b91bee) is the cheaper form of Show: both outcomes are built and one is hidden.\n\nashui's version (ashui/haxe/ashui/ui/For.hx, 53 lines, and Show.hx):\n- each() returns the list and is tracked like a computed; item(value) builds one element; reconciled at the next flush.\n- It is a fragment: no box of its own, its children are laid out as the placing element's own children (LayoutTree.makeFragment), so direction, gap and alignment of that element apply to the rows.\n- Every row is built under its own Owner. A row whose value is still present keeps its element; a row no longer present is disposed, which takes with it what it created.\n- Matching is by == with a linear scan, so it is quadratic in the list, and by reference identity for objects.\n\nWhat Blinc does not have:\n1. A fragment. taffy 0.14 has no display: contents (Display is Block, FlowRoot, Flex, Grid, None), so a wrapper-free region needs either a host form, where the container Div itself owns the region (children from an index on), or a fragment kind in LayoutTree whose children are flattened into the nearest real parent's taffy children. Whatever walks layout_tree.children (paint, hit test, events, the render tree) must see the flattened list.\n2. An owner. Building a row creates signals, computeds, property bindings and keyed state; nothing groups them for disposal, so removed rows leak (compare 483c864). Needs a scope the reactive graph can dispose.\n3. Row identity. StableNodeId is derived from parent id plus sibling index, so inserting or removing in the middle moves every later row's identity. Rows need an explicit key.\n4. In-place splice. The tree can rebuild a parent's children (process_pending_subtree_rebuilds, which patches nodes when the structure is unchanged) but has no insert or remove of one subtree at an index that leaves its siblings alone.\n\nShape proposed: each: Fn() -\u003e impl IntoIterator\u003cItem = T\u003e, tracked like a computed; key: Fn(\u0026T) -\u003e K with K: Hash + Eq (a hash map, not a scan); item: Fn(T) -\u003e impl ElementBuilder, built under a row scope. An item whose key survives keeps its node and its scope; the order is reconciled.\n\nNot started.","files":null}]}